atic, detailed **linguistic map of Ukraine** that remains on screen for the duration. The map uses color-coding to illustrate dialect regions: **red** for Southwestern dialects (western Ukraine), **yellow** for Southeastern dialects (eastern/central Ukraine), and **blue** for Northern Polissian dialects (northwestern border regions). A comprehensive legend identifies numbered sub-dialect regions (1-20), transitional zones with patterned overlays, and surrounding countries (Poland, Belarus, Russia, Romania, Moldova) plus the Black Sea. Geographic labels include major cities like Kyiv, Lviv, and Odesa.\n\nThe mood shifts from energetic cultural celebration to **educational analysis**, suggesting the map serves as a visual aid for narration about Ukraine's linguistic geography and dialect diversity. The extended static display of the map (approximately 20 seconds) indicates a documentary-style explanatory segment following the introductory cultural footage.", 'similarity score': 0.5071065425872803}, {'clip_138_144': 'clip_138_144', 'text': "This video segment transitions from a linguistic map to an aerial cityscape:\n\n**Frames 138s–139s:** A static map displays the geographical distribution of Ukrainian dialects across Ukraine and neighboring regions (Poland, Belarus, Russia, Romania). The map uses color-coded regions (red, blue, yellow, and patterned areas) with numbered labels (1–17) corresponding to a legend in the bottom-left corner identifying specific dialect groups. Major cities like Kyiv, Lviv, Kharkiv, and Odesa are marked, along with the Black Sea and Sea of Azov.\n\n**Frames 140s–144s:** The scene cuts to a static aerial view of **Kyiv** (labeled in yellow text at the bottom). The shot centers on **St. Sophia's Cathedral** with its distinctive green roofs and golden domes, accompanied by its tall white bell tower with a golden cupola. The view captures a large open plaza adjacent to the cathedral, surrounded by trees, historic buildings, and the dense urban